  "body": "## Request\n\nThe maintainer asked for this in chat on 2026-11-23. His words: \"update solidcore fork, and bump git reference in cargo.toml\". He set the priority P2.\n\n## Work\n\n1. Bring our solidcore fork up to date with its upstream. Use a merge, never a force-push. Record the upstream commit that the fork now contains.\n2. Change the git reference of solidcore in the workspace `Cargo.toml` to the new fork commit. Pin a full commit `rev`, not a branch.\n3. Update `Cargo.lock` for solidcore and its new transitive dependencies only.\n4. List each new or changed transitive dependency and its license in the PR body. A new dependency needs the maintainer's decision (`needs-maintainer`).\n5. Keep the third-party notices in `assets/ui/LICENSES.md` current.\n\n## Why effort:high\n\nSolidcore meshes the STEP models (#916, #917). A new mesh can move toolpath pins, as #2849 shows. This is under the safety floor: geometry and toolpaths.\n\n## Checks\n\n- The STEP import tests and the solidcore integration tests.\n- The toolpath pins that use STEP fixtures (for example `crates/app/tests/adaptive_fig8_crossing.rs` and `lead_approach`). Run them targeted, plus one targeted heavy step for the fixtures that moved.\n- Name each pin whose reading moves, and say why. Do not change an assert to hide a change of the toolpath.\n- Check the known-fail tests of #2849. If the new solidcore fixes them, the gate reports them as passing, and #2849 then needs its own decision.\n- Windows, Linux and macOS build from the same source.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Before you claim that solidcore can do something, check its upstream (memory: \"Solidcore replaces truck\").\n- Do the removal of `truck-polymesh` (#3742) in the same PR only if it is trivial. Otherwise leave it in #3742.\n",
